1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,379
This is ECE 358, video 24, the last video: switches, datacenter networks, and a day in the life of a web request. Chapter 6, slides 65 to 100.

2
00:00:14,829 --> 00:00:28,589
You will fill a switch table frame by frame, trace frames through several switches, compare switches and routers, describe datacenter networks, and list every protocol in one web request, in order.

3
00:00:29,039 --> 00:00:36,185
A layer 2 switch, or L2 switch, is a link-layer device. It connects hosts with Ethernet links.

4
00:00:36,635 --> 00:00:40,774
The switch stores each Ethernet frame that arrives, in a buffer.

5
00:00:41,224 --> 00:00:47,859
It examines the destination MAC address. This address is in the link-layer header of the frame.

6
00:00:48,309 --> 00:00:54,858
Then it forwards the frame selectively, on one or more outgoing links. Here it uses one link.

7
00:00:55,308 --> 00:01:01,004
On a half-duplex port, the switch uses CSMA/CD to access the link.

8
00:01:01,454 --> 00:01:10,627
A switch is transparent. The hosts do not know that the switch is there. A host addresses frames to the other host, not to the switch.

9
00:01:11,077 --> 00:01:16,517
A switch is plug-and-play and self-learning. Switches do not need to be configured.

10
00:01:16,967 --> 00:01:20,935
Here is a switch with six interfaces, numbered 1 to 6.

11
00:01:21,385 --> 00:01:24,905
Each host has a dedicated, direct link to the switch.

12
00:01:25,355 --> 00:01:30,262
Ethernet runs on each link in full duplex. So there are no collisions.

13
00:01:30,712 --> 00:01:37,176
So A can send to A prime, and B can send to B prime, at the same time. There is no collision.

14
00:01:37,626 --> 00:01:40,698
Each switch port is its own collision domain.

15
00:01:41,148 --> 00:01:45,607
But A to A prime, and C to A prime, cannot happen at the same time.

16
00:01:46,057 --> 00:01:48,446
Both frames must leave on port 4.

17
00:01:48,896 --> 00:01:52,608
So the switch buffers one frame until port 4 is free.

18
00:01:53,058 --> 00:01:59,885
How does the switch know that A prime is reachable through interface 4, and B prime through interface 5?

19
00:02:00,335 --> 00:02:05,177
Each switch has a switch table. Each entry maps an address to an interface.

20
00:02:05,627 --> 00:02:12,027
An entry has three parts. Part 1 is the MAC address of a host, for example A prime.

21
00:02:12,477 --> 00:02:18,408
Part 2 is the interface that reaches the host. A prime is reached through interface 4.

22
00:02:18,858 --> 00:02:26,538
Part 3 is a TTL, a time to live, here 60. When the TTL ends, the switch removes the entry.

23
00:02:26,988 --> 00:02:29,740
The switch table looks like a routing table.

24
00:02:30,190 --> 00:02:36,249
The slide asks: how are the entries created? Is there something like a routing protocol?

25
00:02:36,699 --> 00:02:43,867
No. The switch needs no routing protocol. It learns the entries itself. The next slide shows how.

26
00:02:44,317 --> 00:02:46,770
The switch table is empty at first.

27
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:53,620
A frame arrives from A. Its source address is A. Its destination address is A prime.

28
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:56,545
The frame arrives on interface 1.

29
00:02:56,995 --> 00:03:00,109
The sender of the frame is A, its source address.

30
00:03:00,559 --> 00:03:06,170
So the switch learns where the sender A is: on the incoming interface, interface 1.

31
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:12,145
The switch records this sender and location pair in its table: A, interface 1.

32
00:03:12,595 --> 00:03:19,891
The entry also gets a TTL of 60. When the TTL ends, the switch removes the entry.

33
00:03:20,341 --> 00:03:29,067
Here is the full algorithm. When a frame arrives: step 1, record the incoming port and the MAC address of the sending host.

34
00:03:29,517 --> 00:03:34,189
Step 2. Index the switch table with the destination MAC address.

35
00:03:34,639 --> 00:03:38,372
Step 3. Check if there is an entry for the destination.

36
00:03:38,822 --> 00:03:44,859
If there is an entry, check if the destination is on the same port that the frame came from.

37
00:03:45,309 --> 00:03:48,851
If yes, drop the frame. The frame does not loop back.

38
00:03:49,301 --> 00:03:52,778
If no, forward the frame on the interface in the entry.

39
00:03:53,228 --> 00:04:00,695
If there is no entry, flood. That is, forward the frame on all interfaces except the arriving interface.

40
00:04:01,145 --> 00:04:06,947
Slide 72. The switch table is empty. Frame 1 goes from A to A prime.

41
00:04:07,397 --> 00:04:13,115
The switch learns that A is on interface 1. It adds the entry A, 1, 60.

42
00:04:13,565 --> 00:04:17,661
A prime is not in the table. So the switch floods the frame.

43
00:04:18,111 --> 00:04:25,620
Flooding sends the frame on ports 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6: all ports except port 1, where it arrived.

44
00:04:26,070 --> 00:04:29,163
Frame 2 is the reply, from A prime to A.

45
00:04:29,613 --> 00:04:35,821
The switch learns that A prime is on interface 4. It adds the entry A prime, 4, 60.

46
00:04:36,271 --> 00:04:42,778
A is in the table, at interface 1. So the switch sends frame 2 on interface 1 only.

47
00:04:43,228 --> 00:04:47,239
So B, C, B prime, and C prime do not see frame 2.

48
00:04:47,689 --> 00:04:56,201
A layer 2 switch has a finite number of ports. Common sizes are 8, 16, 24, and 48 ports.

49
00:04:56,651 --> 00:05:03,968
For hundreds of hosts, you connect switches together. Here four switches, S1 to S4, connect nine hosts.

50
00:05:04,418 --> 00:05:06,679
Host A sends a frame to host G.

51
00:05:07,129 --> 00:05:10,329
S1 must know that G is reached through S4.

52
00:05:10,779 --> 00:05:17,158
S4 must know that G is reached through S3. Then S3 delivers the frame to G.

53
00:05:17,608 --> 00:05:22,088
The switches learn this by self-learning, exactly as with one switch.

54
00:05:22,538 --> 00:05:33,631
Look at the S1 table after frames from D, E, F, G, H, and I. All six hosts are reached through the same interface: the interface to S4.

55
00:05:34,081 --> 00:05:39,543
So many MAC addresses map to the same interface. This is a many-to-1 mapping.

56
00:05:39,993 --> 00:05:47,843
Slide 74. C sends a frame to I. Then I responds to C. All four switch tables start empty.

57
00:05:48,293 --> 00:05:53,264
Frame 1, C to I. S1 learns the sender C: interface to C.

58
00:05:53,714 --> 00:05:59,986
I is not in the S1 table. So S1 floods the frame: to A, B, and S4.

59
00:06:00,436 --> 00:06:04,212
S4 learns the sender C: interface to S1.

60
00:06:04,662 --> 00:06:10,827
I is not in the S4 table. So S4 floods the frame: to S2, and S3.

61
00:06:11,277 --> 00:06:14,968
S2 learns the sender C: interface to S4.

62
00:06:15,418 --> 00:06:20,922
I is not in the S2 table. So S2 floods the frame: to D, E, and F.

63
00:06:21,372 --> 00:06:25,148
S3 learns the sender C: interface to S4.

64
00:06:25,598 --> 00:06:31,614
I is not in the S3 table. So S3 floods the frame: to G, H, and I.

65
00:06:32,064 --> 00:06:33,813
I receives frame 1.

66
00:06:34,263 --> 00:06:42,711
Hosts A, B, D, E, F, G, and H also receive frame 1. Their adapters discard it, because it is for I.

67
00:06:43,161 --> 00:06:48,239
Frame 2, I to C. S3 learns the sender I: interface to I.

68
00:06:48,689 --> 00:06:56,006
C is in the S3 table: interface to S4. So S3 forwards the frame only to S4.

69
00:06:56,456 --> 00:07:00,381
S4 learns the sender I: interface to S3.

70
00:07:00,831 --> 00:07:08,170
C is in the S4 table: interface to S1. So S4 forwards the frame only to S1.

71
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:12,439
S1 learns the sender I: interface to S4.

72
00:07:12,889 --> 00:07:19,587
C is in the S1 table: interface to C. So S1 forwards the frame only to C.

73
00:07:20,037 --> 00:07:23,259
C receives frame 2. No other host sees it.

74
00:07:23,709 --> 00:07:28,039
S2 does not see frame 2. So S2 does not learn I.

75
00:07:28,489 --> 00:07:35,380
Slide 75 shows a small institutional network. The whole network is one IP subnet.

76
00:07:35,830 --> 00:07:41,889
Inside the subnet, switches form a hierarchy: one top switch and three lower switches.

77
00:07:42,339 --> 00:07:46,029
The mail server and the web server connect to the top switch.

78
00:07:46,479 --> 00:07:48,677
The hosts connect to the lower switches.

79
00:07:49,127 --> 00:07:52,327
A router connects the subnet to the external network.

80
00:07:52,777 --> 00:07:59,518
Inside the subnet, switches forward the frames. To leave the subnet, a datagram goes to the router.

81
00:07:59,968 --> 00:08:05,344
Here a datagram goes from a host, through a switch and then a router, to another host.

82
00:08:05,794 --> 00:08:09,677
Switches and routers are both store-and-forward devices.

83
00:08:10,127 --> 00:08:16,953
A switch is a link-layer device. It examines only the link-layer headers, with the MAC addresses.

84
00:08:17,403 --> 00:08:24,614
A router is a network-layer device. It examines the network-layer headers, with the IP addresses.

85
00:08:25,064 --> 00:08:31,485
So the datagram goes up to the network layer in the router, but only up to the link layer in the switch.

86
00:08:31,935 --> 00:08:37,077
Both have forwarding tables. A switch builds its table by flooding and learning.

87
00:08:37,527 --> 00:08:41,132
A router computes its table with routing algorithms.

88
00:08:41,582 --> 00:08:47,065
A switch table holds MAC addresses. A router table holds IP addresses.

89
00:08:47,515 --> 00:08:55,088
Datacenter networks connect tens to hundreds of thousands of hosts. The hosts are closely coupled and close together.

90
00:08:55,538 --> 00:08:58,781
Example 1: e-business, such as Amazon.

91
00:08:59,231 --> 00:09:04,927
Example 2: content servers, such as YouTube, Akamai, Apple, and Microsoft.

92
00:09:05,377 --> 00:09:09,814
Example 3: search engines and data mining, such as Google.

93
00:09:10,264 --> 00:09:12,995
Datacenter networks have three challenges.

94
00:09:13,445 --> 00:09:19,034
Challenge 1: many applications, and each one serves massive numbers of clients.

95
00:09:19,484 --> 00:09:24,177
Challenge 2: reliability. The communication must be fail-safe.

96
00:09:24,627 --> 00:09:30,601
Challenge 3: balance the load, to avoid processing, networking, and data bottlenecks.

97
00:09:31,051 --> 00:09:38,496
From the bottom up: server racks. Each rack holds 20 to 40 server blades. The blades are the hosts.

98
00:09:38,946 --> 00:09:48,226
Each rack has one top of rack switch, or TOR switch. It connects to the blades at 40 to 100 gigabits per second.

99
00:09:48,676 --> 00:09:55,332
Each tier-2 switch connects to about 16 TOR switches below it. The drawing shows 4.

100
00:09:55,782 --> 00:10:00,454
Each tier-1 switch connects to about 16 tier-2 switches below it.

101
00:10:00,904 --> 00:10:05,085
At the top, border routers connect the datacenter to the outside.

102
00:10:05,535 --> 00:10:11,573
Slide 80 shows a real example, the Facebook F16 datacenter topology.

103
00:10:12,023 --> 00:10:20,001
This is a smaller version of slide 81, with 4 tier-1 switches, 4 tier-2 switches, and 8 racks.

104
00:10:20,451 --> 00:10:23,971
Each tier-2 switch connects to every tier-1 switch.

105
00:10:24,421 --> 00:10:27,109
Each rack connects to two tier-2 switches.

106
00:10:27,559 --> 00:10:34,109
So there are many paths between two racks. Here is one path from rack 1 to rack 6, in red.

107
00:10:34,559 --> 00:10:36,671
Here is a second path, in blue.

108
00:10:37,121 --> 00:10:41,515
The two paths use different switches. So the two paths are disjoint.

109
00:10:41,965 --> 00:10:45,784
Multiple paths increase the throughput and the reliability.

110
00:10:46,234 --> 00:10:49,498
A load balancer does application-layer routing.

111
00:10:49,948 --> 00:10:52,977
It receives the requests of the external clients.

112
00:10:53,427 --> 00:10:57,246
It directs the workload to servers inside the datacenter.

113
00:10:57,696 --> 00:11:00,619
It returns the results to the external client.

114
00:11:01,069 --> 00:11:04,610
So the client does not see the inside of the datacenter.

115
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:16,025
At the link layer: RoCE, remote DMA over converged Ethernet. Data moves directly between the memories of two servers, without their CPUs.

116
00:11:16,475 --> 00:11:33,969
At the transport layer: explicit congestion notification, ECN, is used in congestion control, for example in DCTCP and DCQCN. There are also tests with hop-by-hop, or backpressure, congestion control.

117
00:11:34,419 --> 00:11:40,264
For routing and management, SDN is widely used within and among datacenters.

118
00:11:40,714 --> 00:11:48,394
Related services and data are placed as close as possible, for example in the same rack or a nearby rack.

119
00:11:48,844 --> 00:11:53,281
This minimizes the traffic through the tier-2 and tier-1 switches.

120
00:11:53,731 --> 00:12:02,670
Now we put it all together. One simple scenario uses the protocols of every layer. The network is from slide 93.

121
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:05,296
On the left is the school network.

122
00:12:05,746 --> 00:12:08,818
The Comcast network holds the DNS server.

123
00:12:09,268 --> 00:12:11,743
Google's network holds the web server.

124
00:12:12,193 --> 00:12:15,627
A student attaches a laptop to the school network.

125
00:12:16,077 --> 00:12:21,944
Then the student requests the web page www.google.com.

126
00:12:22,394 --> 00:12:32,015
Slide 86 shows the whole course in one picture. On the laptop, the DHCP client runs on UDP port 68.

127
00:12:32,465 --> 00:12:37,287
The web browser runs HTTP on TCP, from a free port.

128
00:12:37,737 --> 00:12:43,646
On the servers: the DHCP server runs on UDP port 67.

129
00:12:44,096 --> 00:12:48,213
The DNS server runs on UDP port 53.

130
00:12:48,663 --> 00:12:52,653
The web server runs HTTP on TCP port 80.

131
00:12:53,103 --> 00:12:59,737
Under each application are IP at the network layer, then the link layer, then the physical layer.

132
00:13:00,187 --> 00:13:04,497
Between them are switches that learn, the first-hop router, and ARP.

133
00:13:04,947 --> 00:13:13,501
Routing works inside each autonomous system with OSPF or RIP, and between autonomous systems with BGP.

134
00:13:13,951 --> 00:13:29,269
Step 1. The arriving laptop needs its own IP address, the IP address of the first-hop router, the IP address of the DNS server, and the network mask. It gets all of them with DHCP.

135
00:13:29,719 --> 00:13:38,039
The DHCP request is put in a UDP segment, then in an IP datagram, then in an Ethernet frame.

136
00:13:38,489 --> 00:13:47,001
The DHCP client uses UDP port 68. The DHCP server uses port 67.

137
00:13:47,451 --> 00:13:50,907
The IP destination is the broadcast address.

138
00:13:51,357 --> 00:13:55,815
The frame's destination MAC address is the broadcast address too.

139
00:13:56,265 --> 00:13:59,060
The switch floods the broadcast frame on the LAN.

140
00:13:59,510 --> 00:14:03,649
At the same time, the switch learns the interface of the laptop.

141
00:14:04,099 --> 00:14:08,600
The router runs the DHCP server. It receives the frame.

142
00:14:09,050 --> 00:14:19,354
The router demultiplexes the frame: Ethernet to IP, IP to UDP, and UDP to DHCP, at port 67.

143
00:14:19,804 --> 00:14:33,735
The DHCP server replies with a DHCP ACK. The ACK holds the client's IP address, the first-hop router's IP address, and the DNS server's name and IP address.

144
00:14:34,185 --> 00:14:40,414
The switch forwards the ACK only to the laptop. It learned the laptop's interface from the request.

145
00:14:40,864 --> 00:14:47,840
Now the laptop has its IP address. It also knows its first-hop router and the DNS server.

146
00:14:48,290 --> 00:14:57,463
Step 2. The laptop needs the IP address of www.google.com. So it uses DNS.

147
00:14:57,913 --> 00:15:04,868
The laptop puts the DNS query in UDP, then in IP, then in an Ethernet frame.

148
00:15:05,318 --> 00:15:11,291
To send this frame to the router, the laptop needs the MAC address of the router interface.

149
00:15:11,741 --> 00:15:16,328
So the laptop runs ARP. It broadcasts an ARP query on the LAN.

150
00:15:16,778 --> 00:15:18,954
The router receives the ARP query.

151
00:15:19,404 --> 00:15:25,953
The router replies with an ARP reply. The reply gives the MAC address of the router interface.

152
00:15:26,403 --> 00:15:30,627
Now the laptop can send the frame that holds the DNS query.

153
00:15:31,077 --> 00:15:38,608
Step 3. The laptop sends the DNS query to UDP port 53 on the DNS server.

154
00:15:39,058 --> 00:15:45,415
The laptop knows the IP address of the DNS server from DHCP, in step 1.

155
00:15:45,865 --> 00:15:50,580
The LAN switch forwards the frame from the laptop to the first-hop router.

156
00:15:51,030 --> 00:15:56,918
The datagram goes from the school network into the Comcast network, to the DNS server.

157
00:15:57,368 --> 00:16:04,685
The routers forward it with tables that RIP, OSPF, IS-IS, or BGP created.

158
00:16:05,135 --> 00:16:10,085
At the DNS server, the datagram is demultiplexed to DNS.

159
00:16:10,535 --> 00:16:25,788
The DNS server replies to the client with the IP address of www.google.com: 64 dot 233 dot 169 dot 105.

160
00:16:26,238 --> 00:16:33,705
Step 4. Before it sends the HTTP request, the browser asks TCP to open a connection.

161
00:16:34,155 --> 00:16:40,896
The connection goes from the laptop's IP address and port, to the web server's IP address, port 80.

162
00:16:41,346 --> 00:16:46,466
TCP sends a SYN segment. This is step 1 of the three-way handshake.

163
00:16:46,916 --> 00:16:53,423
The SYN is routed between domains: from the school network, through Comcast, into Google's network.

164
00:16:53,873 --> 00:16:58,587
The web server responds with a SYN plus ACK segment. This is step 2.

165
00:16:59,037 --> 00:17:02,472
The laptop answers with an ACK. This is step 3.

166
00:17:02,922 --> 00:17:05,845
Now the TCP connection is established.

167
00:17:06,295 --> 00:17:15,255
Step 5. The browser sends the HTTP request into the TCP socket. It asks for index.htm.

168
00:17:15,705 --> 00:17:23,427
The IP datagram with the HTTP request is routed to www.google.com.

169
00:17:23,877 --> 00:17:32,539
The web server puts the file index.htm in an HTTP reply. It asks TCP to send the reply.

170
00:17:32,989 --> 00:17:39,559
TCP puts the reply in a segment. The network layer puts the segment in an IP packet.

171
00:17:40,009 --> 00:17:45,044
The IP packet is routed back through the autonomous systems to the laptop.

172
00:17:45,494 --> 00:17:49,441
The browser receives the reply and displays the web page.

173
00:17:49,891 --> 00:17:59,725
Question bank. Question 56: what is the purpose of a switch table? Question 57: what is its structure? Pause the video and try it.

174
00:18:00,175 --> 00:18:06,746
Answer to 56. The switch table tells the switch on which interface to forward each frame.

175
00:18:07,196 --> 00:18:15,495
So the switch forwards a frame only on the interface of the entry. A frame to A prime leaves only on interface 4.

176
00:18:15,945 --> 00:18:23,027
If the destination is on the interface where the frame arrived, the switch drops the frame. This is filtering.

177
00:18:23,477 --> 00:18:30,048
Answer to 57. Each entry has three parts. Part 1 is the MAC address of a host.

178
00:18:30,498 --> 00:18:33,613
Part 2 is the interface that reaches the host.

179
00:18:34,063 --> 00:18:39,951
Part 3 is a TTL. When the TTL ends, the switch removes the entry.

180
00:18:40,401 --> 00:18:44,475
So each entry is: MAC address, interface, TTL.

181
00:18:44,925 --> 00:18:55,485
Question 58: how does a switch construct its table? Question 59: which mapping best describes a switch table? Pause the video and try it.

182
00:18:55,935 --> 00:19:05,407
Answer to 58. By self-learning. Example: a frame from D arrives at S1, on the interface of S1 to S4.

183
00:19:05,857 --> 00:19:09,207
S1 records the MAC address of the sender: D.

184
00:19:09,657 --> 00:19:14,862
S1 also records the incoming interface: the interface to S4.

185
00:19:15,312 --> 00:19:19,600
The switch needs no configuration and no routing protocol.

186
00:19:20,050 --> 00:19:27,751
Answer to 59. After frames from E, F, G, H, and I, the S1 table has six entries.

187
00:19:28,201 --> 00:19:33,044
All six entries map to the same interface: the interface to S4.

188
00:19:33,494 --> 00:19:42,497
Each MAC address maps to only one interface, but many MAC addresses map to the same interface. So the mapping is many-to-1.

189
00:19:42,947 --> 00:19:50,819
Question 60 gives the algorithm of slide 71 with gaps. Fill in the gaps. Pause the video and try it.

190
00:19:51,269 --> 00:19:54,298
Gap 1: when a frame is received at the switch.

191
00:19:54,748 --> 00:19:59,676
Gap 2: record the incoming link and the MAC address of the sending host.

192
00:20:00,126 --> 00:20:04,542
Gap 3: index the switch table with the destination MAC address.

193
00:20:04,992 --> 00:20:08,427
Gap 4: if an entry is found for the destination,

194
00:20:08,877 --> 00:20:15,426
Gap 5: then, if the destination is on the port that the frame came from, drop the frame.

195
00:20:15,876 --> 00:20:20,441
Gap 6: else, forward the frame on the interface in the entry.

196
00:20:20,891 --> 00:20:28,145
Gap 7: if no entry is found, flood: forward on all interfaces except the arriving interface.

197
00:20:28,595 --> 00:20:38,984
Question 61: what is flooding? Question 62: an L2 switch floods in two situations. What are they? Pause the video and try it.

198
00:20:39,434 --> 00:20:48,650
Answer to 61. Flooding means: the switch forwards the frame on all interfaces, except the interface where the frame arrived.

199
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:56,375
Here the frame arrived on interface 1. So it goes out on interfaces 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.

200
00:20:56,825 --> 00:21:08,643
Answer to 62. Situation 1: the destination MAC address is not in the switch table. An example is the first frame to A prime on slide 72.

201
00:21:09,093 --> 00:21:17,669
Situation 2: the destination is the broadcast address, for example in an ARP query or a DHCP request.

202
00:21:18,119 --> 00:21:28,466
No frame has the broadcast address as its source. So the broadcast address is never in the table, step 3 finds no entry, and the switch floods.

203
00:21:28,916 --> 00:21:48,159
Question 63. You must connect 1000 hosts. Each L2 switch has 48 ports. How many switches do you need? Show a layout that minimizes the delay to an external server. There is no published answer, so we use the slides. Pause the video and try it.

204
00:21:48,609 --> 00:21:54,795
Count the ports. Each switch has 48 ports. So n switches have 48 n ports.

205
00:21:55,245 --> 00:21:57,464
The hosts need 1000 ports.

206
00:21:57,914 --> 00:22:01,242
The router to the external server needs 1 port.

207
00:22:01,692 --> 00:22:13,639
The switches connect in a tree. A tree of n switches has n minus 1 links. Each link uses 2 ports, one at each end. With 4 switches: 3 links, so 6 ports.

208
00:22:14,089 --> 00:22:19,657
So 48 n must be at least 1000, plus 1, plus 2 n minus 2.

209
00:22:20,107 --> 00:22:31,435
Take 2 n from both sides: 46 n. Take 2 from 1001: 999. So 46 n is at least 999.

210
00:22:31,885 --> 00:22:40,994
999 divided by 46 is 21.7. n is a whole number, so n is at least 22.

211
00:22:41,444 --> 00:22:52,751
Check with 21 switches. They have 21 times 48, which is 1008 ports. But 1041 ports are needed. 33 ports are missing.

212
00:22:53,201 --> 00:23:04,230
With 22 switches: 22 times 48 is 1056 ports. 1043 ports are needed. So 13 ports are spare.

213
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:10,888
Now the layout, with 22 switches. The delay to the external server must be small.

214
00:23:11,338 --> 00:23:13,685
One root switch connects to the router.

215
00:23:14,135 --> 00:23:21,687
21 access switches connect to the root. 1 root plus 21 access switches is 22 switches.

216
00:23:22,137 --> 00:23:30,627
Each access switch uses 1 port for its uplink to the root. 48 minus 1 leaves 47 ports for hosts.

217
00:23:31,077 --> 00:23:37,243
21 access switches times 47 hosts is 987 hosts.

218
00:23:37,693 --> 00:23:48,167
The root uses 21 ports for the access switches and 1 port for the router. 48 minus 21 minus 1 leaves 26 free ports.

219
00:23:48,617 --> 00:24:03,316
1000 minus 987 is 13. So 13 hosts go on the root switch, and all 1000 hosts fit. 26 minus 13 leaves 13 spare ports, as in the port count.

220
00:24:03,766 --> 00:24:10,337
For the delay: a frame from any host reaches the router through at most 2 store-and-forward switches.

221
00:24:10,787 --> 00:24:16,291
A chain of switches would put many switches on the path, and each switch adds a delay.

222
00:24:16,741 --> 00:24:33,807
Question 64 uses the network of slide 73. All four switch tables start empty. C sends a frame to I. Then F sends a frame to C. Part a: show the four switch tables at the end. Pause the video and try it.

223
00:24:34,257 --> 00:24:43,303
Frame 1, C to I, is the same as on slide 74. Every switch learns the sender C, on the interface toward C.

224
00:24:43,753 --> 00:24:47,699
No switch knows I. So every switch floods frame 1.

225
00:24:48,149 --> 00:24:53,333
Frame 2, F to C. S2 learns the sender F: interface to F.

226
00:24:53,783 --> 00:25:00,887
C is in the S2 table: interface to S4. So S2 forwards the frame only to S4.

227
00:25:01,337 --> 00:25:05,177
S4 learns the sender F: interface to S2.

228
00:25:05,627 --> 00:25:12,966
C is in the S4 table: interface to S1. So S4 forwards the frame only to S1.

229
00:25:13,416 --> 00:25:17,384
S1 learns the sender F: interface to S4.

230
00:25:17,834 --> 00:25:24,533
C is in the S1 table: interface to C. So S1 forwards the frame only to C.

231
00:25:24,983 --> 00:25:29,249
So S1, S2, and S4 have two entries each: C and F.

232
00:25:29,699 --> 00:25:34,094
S3 has only C, because frame 2 never reaches S3.

233
00:25:34,544 --> 00:25:38,192
No switch learns I, because I never sends a frame.

234
00:25:38,642 --> 00:25:47,239
Part b: how many frames actually reach the hardware interfaces of hosts A, D, and G? Pause the video and try it.

235
00:25:47,689 --> 00:25:53,108
Frame 1, C to I. No switch knows I, so every switch floods frame 1.

236
00:25:53,558 --> 00:25:56,481
So frame 1 reaches A, through S1.

237
00:25:56,931 --> 00:25:59,064
It reaches D, through S2.

238
00:25:59,514 --> 00:26:02,010
And it reaches G, through S3.

239
00:26:02,460 --> 00:26:11,335
Frame 2, F to C. Every switch on the way knows C. So frame 2 goes only on the path S2, S4, S1, to C.

240
00:26:11,785 --> 00:26:14,921
So frame 2 does not reach A, D, or G.

241
00:26:15,371 --> 00:26:23,520
In total, exactly one frame reaches each of A, D, and G. Their adapters discard it, because it is for I.

242
00:26:23,970 --> 00:26:28,983
Three synthesis questions that use this video. Pause the video and try them.

243
00:26:29,433 --> 00:26:34,681
Question 1. A network-layer protocol is present on hosts and routers.

244
00:26:35,131 --> 00:26:43,942
It is not present on links or on L2 switches. Slide 76 shows that a switch goes up only to the link layer.

245
00:26:44,392 --> 00:26:48,211
Question 2. DHCP uses UDP.

246
00:26:48,661 --> 00:26:52,031
The DHCP client uses port 68.

247
00:26:52,481 --> 00:26:56,065
The DHCP server uses port 67.

248
00:26:56,515 --> 00:27:05,027
Question 3. First, DHCP: the laptop gets its own IP address, the router, and the DNS server.

249
00:27:05,477 --> 00:27:07,888
Then ARP, for the router's MAC address.

250
00:27:08,338 --> 00:27:14,845
Then DNS, for the IP address of www.google.com.

251
00:27:15,295 --> 00:27:18,943
Then the TCP handshake: SYN, SYN ack, ACK.

252
00:27:19,393 --> 00:27:22,763
Then HTTP: the request and the reply.

253
00:27:23,213 --> 00:27:32,472
To recap. A switch is a transparent, plug-and-play, store-and-forward link-layer device. Each port is its own collision domain.

254
00:27:32,922 --> 00:27:47,173
The switch table maps MAC addresses to interfaces, many-to-1. The switch learns from the sender of each frame. It forwards to a known destination, and floods for an unknown or broadcast destination.

255
00:27:47,623 --> 00:27:55,303
A router uses IP addresses and routing algorithms. A switch uses MAC addresses and self-learning.

256
00:27:55,753 --> 00:28:03,795
A datacenter connects racks through top of rack, tier-2, and tier-1 switches, with many paths and a load balancer.

257
00:28:04,245 --> 00:28:17,707
And one web request uses DHCP, ARP, DNS, TCP, and HTTP, in that order. This completes ECE 358. Good luck on the exam.
